{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>There shall not be to you, etc.</b> Meaning: because He preceded with a commandment concerning faith in Him, may He be blessed, and that is a matter whose essence is in the heart — therefore He said that likewise one must distance from his heart any divinity other than Him, even if he does not express it with his lips. This is what is intimated by the word <b>לְךָ</b> ('to you') — meaning, even in your thought. Accordingly, we find a warning against thought of idolatry, as they said, of blessed memory (Kiddushin 39b), from the verse (Ezekiel 14:5): ״לְמַעַן תְּפֹשׂ״ etc. — that HaShem punishes for thought of idolatry. And they said, of blessed memory (Zevaḥim 106b): there is no punishment without a warning — here then is its warning." "<b>Further,</b> it intends to say that all idolatry in the world — the action of a non-Jewish worshiper does not produce an effectual degree in the worshipped entity to make it a god, as the action of an Israelite does, for he invests great power in the aspect of evil and overpowers, heaven forbid, the superior aspect before the aspect of evil, may the Merciful One save us. This is what He says: <b>לֹא יִהְיֶה לְךָ אֱלֹהִים</b> — meaning, your act of bringing-it-into-being will make it elohim." "<b>Further,</b> it intends [to say] that through the mediation of the worshiper, he brings into being an existence that is elohim — something that did not exist beforehand." "<b>Further,</b> it intends to say that a strange el that a man makes other than Him, may He be blessed, is itself the one that exacts vengeance from its worshipper, in the manner of what He said (Jeremiah 2:19): ״תְּיַסְּרֵךְ רָעָתֵךְ״ ('your own wickedness shall reprove you')." "<b>Further,</b> it intends [to say] that when he makes an el other than Him, may He be blessed, it is impossible for him to have only one; and even though he does not wish to worship but one, he will end up worshipping many, for there is no 'one.' This is what He says: <b>לֹא יִהְיֶה</b> — in the singular — and concludes by saying <b>אֱלֹהִים אֲחֵרִים</b> ('other elohim,' plural). Or it may intend to say that when he begins to worship one, in the end he will worship many. Go out and learn from the words of the prophets of Israel (Sanhedrin 102b) the proliferation of deities that Israel worshipped in the land, in their sins." "And its saying <b>upon My face</b> is explained according to what Rambam wrote in chapter 9 of Hilkhot Yesodei haTorah: a prophet is to be believed in his words if he says to transgress one commandment from the commandments of the Torah temporarily, by the word of HaShem — but specifically other commandments, excluding idolatry, in which case even temporarily he is a false prophet and liable to the death penalty. And this is the meaning of <b>upon My face</b>: in all particular times, for He had already prefaced by making known His name — that He was and is and will be without interruption — and from now on He has brought into the scope of the prohibition all the days and hours and moments, that all of them are included in the prohibition of ״לֹא יִהְיֶה״." "<b>Further</b>, it intends by saying <b>upon My face</b> — a hint to remove the claim of the sect of errants who practice idolatry through intermediaries, claiming the negation of His existence, heaven forbid. Therefore He said ״upon My face,״ meaning: everything that exists in the world is before My face, for the eyes of HaShem range throughout all the earth." "<b>Further</b>, it intends to give a reason for His warning, for according to human intellect the matter seems far-fetched, for every worshipper worships only one of His attendants, blessed be He, and on the contrary — from the greatness of the servant the greatness of the master becomes known. And if so, one who worships the sun and the moon is worshipping His servants and attendants, blessed be He — so why should HaShem be insistent about the matter? Therefore He said <b>upon My face</b>, meaning: for the reason that I become angered by the matter that they make a god of what is no god. And the word ״פָּנַי״ is in the language of anger, as in its saying (Exodus 33:14) ״פָּנַי יֵלֵכוּ״ etc., and as He said at the end: ״כִּי אָנֹכִי״ etc. ״אֵל קַנָּא״." "<b>Further</b>, it intends <b>upon My face</b> — because of the matter of My face, so that you not prevent yourselves from gazing toward Elohim, for there is no pleasure in the world like seeing the face of HaShem in the world of souls, for this is the ultimate hope. And HaShem warned them lest they bring about their own prevention from this apprehension." "<b>Further</b>, it intends according to its saying (Deuteronomy 28:10) ״וְרָאוּ כָּל עַמֵּי הָאָרֶץ כִּי שֵׁם ה׳ נִקְרָא עָלֶיךָ,״ and from now on, when one worships other elohim, behold he rests an aspect of evil upon the face upon which holiness has established its place."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:30:53.452761007Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}